{
    "repo": "systemd/systemd",
    "pr_number": 31827,
    "lang": "c",
    "pr_title": "Reduce superfluous dnssec transactions",
    "pr_body": "This dramatically reduces the number of transactions and round trips used to retrieve the necessary material for dnssec validation. Eliminating these transactions results in a massive speedup resolving beneath insecure delegations, which are far and away the most common case \u2014 only a tiny fraction of a fraction of internet domains are actually signed.\r\n\r\nsd-resolved exhibited particularly poor performance for names with many dns labels, like the reverse nybble format used in ip6.arpa. A typical reverse lookup performed by ping -6 google.com could take well over _10 seconds_ to complete from cold-cache, even on a fast network, and even with a hot-cache forwarding resolver on the lan. In at least one case, this patch reduces the number of dns transactions used for such a lookup from 67 to 11, speeding up resolution 10x in the process, because the eliminated superfluous transactions are also the ones not likely to be present in the recursive resolver's own cache.\r\n\r\nWe also speculatively request DS records along with DNSKEY records, since we will almost certainly need them. Overall, the new method should be faster by some amount in every case.\r\n\r\nI think it is correct, at least I don't know of any domains that are incorrectly accepted/rejected with the new code, but it is probably worth some careful review. If you can find/create a domain that doesn't validate correctly, please let me know and hopefully we can make a test case for it.\n\nFixes: #31826",
    "pr_comments": [
        "Thank you for looking into this. Any chance this fixes https://github.com/systemd/systemd/issues/9867 too?",
        "@jiripospisil Yeah.\r\n\r\nI can't actually reproduce with savannah.gnu.org on 255.4, and the logs are from too old a version of sd-resolved for me to recognize what went wrong. Manually querying, I don't see anything about that name that I know the old algorithm would trip over either. So I think that one had already been resolved, actually.\r\n\r\nsts.sky.blackbaud.com, the most recent name mentioned in a comment, appears to be a dupe of #24171 though.",
        "@bluca Can you check if some of the linked issues can be closed? Basically, all of the above are dupes of #24171 or #22131 I think. I resolved them by accident in 47690634f157. I hadn't previously considered this, but some of those superfluous queries could (legitimately) fail, causing us to reject valid answers.",
        "> @bluca Can you check if some of the linked issues can be closed? Basically, all of the above are dupes of #24171 or #22131 I think. I resolved them by accident in [4769063](https://github.com/systemd/systemd/commit/47690634f157150e7b69c832d1f2d64d18b3f124). I hadn't previously considered this, but some of those superfluous queries could (legitimately) fail, causing us to reject valid answers.\r\n\r\nAfraid I am not a DNS expert by any meaning of the term - happy to close them if you tell me they are dups and solved by the recent changes",
        "@yuwata thanks, I made #31974 for those.\r\n\r\n@bluca Okay, well, I'm pretty sure they are resolved. The failing domains all have some way to convince sd-resolved it needed to find an SOA somewhere one doesn't exist. I didn't look too carefully at each one, especially since the dnssec status may have changed over time, but they don't reproduce after this PR."
    ],
